4. On January 2010 a new Import Policy Order 2009-2012 introduced under the Section 3 (1) of the Imports and Exports (Control) Act-1950.&nbsp; 

 5. New policy in Sub-Rule 29 of Rule 25 extended (including ceramic tiles definition, classification characteristics in the list) the list of the products that need clearance certificate from the BSTI. Only after BSTI clearance importers are allowed to pay taxes to custom authority and can release the goods from the port, even though the goods inspected by the Government authorized agent in the place of shipment, under the PSI Rules framed under Section 25A of Customs Act, same also being reiterated in the Import Policy declared under Section 3(1) of Import of the Imports and Ex-ports (Control) Act-1950.&nbsp; 

 16. Respondent No. 1 filed an affidavit-in-opposition controverting the statements made in the Writ Petition. The case of this respondent, in short, is that pre-shipment agency only testes quantity, description, classification and price of the goods and no quality is tested by them to show whether the quality of the goods is according to BDS ISO standard or not. In fact, PSI companies are not properly equipped to early out the test as such. According to Section 3(1) of the Import and Export (Control) Act, 1950 the Government is empowered to make import Policy. Accordingly, the Government made Import Policy Order 2009-2012 and included therein the ceramic tiles at serial No. 37 of Sub-Rule 29 of Rule 25 for certification by BSTI whether the goods are made as per standard prescribed by Bangladesh. On 30.7.2006, the council of Bangladesh Standards and Testing Institute at its 18th meeting took a decision to propose to the Government for mandatory certification of 8 kinds of goods. On 2.7.2007, the Government published 2 SROs under section 24(1) and 24(2) of Bangladesh Standard and Testing Institution Ordinance, 1985 prohibiting sale and market of ceramic goods which are below Bangladesh standard and mandatory standard marking on the body of the goods. After that, the Government in its Import Policy order 2009-2012 included ceramic tiles for testing their quality by BSTI. Earlier the ceramic tiles were not used in large scale but at present almost every flat owner has been using ceramic tiles. Therefore, there is a demand from Bangladeshi consumers to take a preventive measures checking the quality of tiles by the Government so that they cannot be cheated.&nbsp; 

 21. We have considered the Writ Petition and its annexures, supplementary affidavit and the affidavit-in-opposition filed by respondent Nos. 1 and 2 and their annexures. Admittedly, according to section 3(1) of the Imports and Exports (Control) Act, 1950 the Government is empowered to make Import Policy Order. Accordingly, the Government has made Import Policy Order 2009-2012 including ceramic tiles at serial No. 37 of Sub-Rule 29 of Rule 25 for certification by BSTI to certain whether the goods are made as per Bangladesh Standard (BDS) prescribed by Bangladesh. On 30.7.2006, the council of Bangladesh Standards and Testing Institutes at its 18th meeting took a decision proposing to the Government for mandatory certification of 8 kinds of goods and the Government published 2 SRO on 2.7.2007 under section 24(1) and 24(2) of Bangladesh Standard and Testing Institution Ordinance, 1985 prohibiting selling or marketing tiles which are below Bangladesh Standard (BDS) and for mandatory standard marking on the body of the goods as evidenced by Annexure-Ka, Kha and Ga respectively to the affidavit-in-opposition of respondent No. 1.&nbsp;
